-- Q13493: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 385 and judicial debt-equity factors, courts evaluate debt-to-equity ratio, fixed maturity, unconditional repayment, and other indicia of genuine indebtedness. A 40:1 ratio ($2,000,000 / $50,000) constitutes extreme thin capitalization, and earnings-contingent repayment without a fixed maturity strongly indicates equity. The loan is recharacterized as equity, and interest payments become nondeductible constructive dividends.
Wrong (B): Shareholder loans are not always respected. Courts apply a multi-factor substance-over-form analysis, and extreme thin capitalization with contingent repayment warrants reclassification.
Wrong (C): When the entire advance is reclassified as equity, both interest and principal repayments are recharacterized as distributions, not selectively disallowed.
Wrong (D): There is no requirement for immediate repayment to avoid reclassification. The analysis turns on the totality of circumstances at the time of the advance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13493;

-- Q13495: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 316 and the constructive dividend doctrine, when a C corporation provides personal use of corporate property to a controlling shareholder, the FMV of personal use is a constructive dividend. The personal-use portion is 80% x $15,000 = $12,000, which is dividend income to Marcus. The corporation loses that deduction under Section 162(a) because personal use is not an ordinary and necessary business expense.
Wrong (A): Personal use of corporate property by a controlling shareholder is not a tax-free fringe benefit. Fringe benefit exclusions under Section 132 generally do not apply to more-than-2% shareholders of S corporations or controlling C corporation shareholders for personal-use property.
Wrong (B): Only the personal-use portion ($12,000) is disallowed. The 20% business-use portion ($3,000) remains deductible under Section 162.
Wrong (D): There is no requirement for reimbursement to maintain shareholder status. The consequence is constructive dividend treatment, not loss of ownership.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13495;

-- Q13500: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 301 and constructive dividend principles, when a C corporation with sufficient E&P pays personal expenses of a shareholder without business purpose, the payments are constructive dividends. The $35,000 vacation and $18,000 tuition total $53,000 of dividend income to Grayson, and Falcon Corp cannot deduct these amounts under Section 162 because they lack a business purpose.
Wrong (A): Personal expenses of a shareholder are not deductible business expenses under Section 162. They lack the ordinary and necessary business purpose required for deductibility.
Wrong (B): Tuition payments for a shareholder''s children without a business purpose are not excludable fringe benefits. Both payments are treated as constructive dividends from accumulated E&P.
Wrong (C): Corporations cannot make nontaxable gifts to shareholders. Payments from a C corporation with E&P to a shareholder are distributions under Section 301, not gifts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13500;

-- Q13508: Owner-Entity Transactions [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 301, a constructive dividend is an economic benefit conferred on a shareholder without a formal dividend declaration, such as personal expense payments, bargain sales, or rent-free use of corporate property. The IRS recharacterizes the benefit as a dividend to the extent of E&P regardless of the form chosen by the parties.
Wrong (A): A formally declared dividend is not a constructive dividend. Constructive dividends arise from informal or undisclosed benefits, not board-authorized distributions.
Wrong (B): A constructive dividend is not limited to accumulated E&P distributions. It is defined by the manner of conferral (informal benefit), not the source of funds.
Wrong (C): A return of capital distribution exceeding basis is treated as capital gain, not as a constructive dividend. These are distinct concepts under Section 301.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13508;

-- Q13509: Owner-Entity Transactions [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the multi-factor debt-equity analysis following IRC Section 385, the totality of circumstances determines classification. The 1.25:1 debt-to-equity ratio, written promissory note, 7-year fixed maturity, AFR interest rate, and consistent repayment history on the prior identical loan all support debt treatment. While subordination and earnings-contingent suspension are negative factors, they are outweighed by the strong positive indicia.
Wrong (B): Although subordination and earnings-contingent payments are negative factors, the overall balance of factors favors debt. No single factor is dispositive in the multi-factor analysis.
Wrong (C): There is no 1:1 debt-to-equity limit that automatically splits an advance into debt and equity components. The analysis is holistic, not mechanical.
Wrong (D): Sole shareholder loans are not automatically reclassified as equity. Courts apply the same multi-factor analysis regardless of ownership percentage.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13509;

